WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.480
A new study reveals ai already handles up to half the work scientists do when running real research projects. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 220. Next comes how Claude tighties its own notes in quiet hours, a budget map of chatbots you can test, and quick bits. Researchers at Anthropic, the company behind the chatbot Claude, track 207,000 real sessions of lab and analysis work. Working scientists use Claude, a program you type to in ordinary language,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.400
and a more technical version called Claude code. Think of a science fair poster.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.540
You gather measurements, run the calculations, write the report, and then decide what the numbers actually mean.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.960
That is the same pile of chores these scientists were handing to artificial intelligence or AI,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.780
which just means software that can read, write, and find patterns.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:57.040
Current tools could already take over between 27 and 52% of the total researcher time needed to finish a study.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.220
with the middle value across all fields landing at 38%.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.960
Picture a lab partner who lives inside a spreadsheet

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.600
and also drafts the write-up

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.400
while you still choose the question

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.240
and judge whether the answer makes sense.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:10.660
That is the split.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.080
Most of the help shows up in two places.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.000
Analyzing data accounts for roughly one-third

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.440
of ordinary cloud sessions

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.020
and nearly half of cloud code sessions.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.880
And the other big chunk is writing the final report.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.160
A session here is just one stretch of work in the chat

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.240
like one long conversation while you chip away at a project. Computer science,

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.520
mathematics, and statistics researchers turn to the tool far more often than

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.840
researchers in health or lab-based physical sciences, because those formal

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.820
fields contain more of the tasks AI currently handles well. It is like a video game

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.880
where some levels already match the power up you are holding, and other levels

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.000
still need hands, glassware, and a real bench. When the same scientists were asked in a

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.160
survey which parts of research matter most. They ranked coming up with fresh ideas at the top.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:04.600
Yet right now they ask Claude for idea generation less often than they ask it to crunch numbers

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:10.040
or polish sentences. That is the difference between asking a classmate to invent a new experiment

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:15.400
versus asking them to check your arithmetic and clean up your paragraph. On a special version

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:21.360
called Claude Science, the share of idea generation requests doubles, showing that purpose-built tools

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.500
can shift how people use AI. Tasks AI is theoretically strongest that receive 2-4 times

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.280
more real use than their share of a researcher's day would predict, while tasks involving

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.580
physical work receive almost none. In fields where many tasks line up with AI strengths,

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:44.800
overall usage rises. In fields where fewer tasks match, usage stays low. Using a simple model,

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:49.560
the researchers estimate that widespread adoption of today's capabilities could roughly double

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.800
research output in computer science and statistics while producing smaller but still noticeable

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.980
speedups in chemistry or anthropology. For any student thinking about a future in science,

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.020
the numbers suggest that the daily rhythm of lab work is already changing,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.100
with more time-freeze for the parts of research that still need human judgment.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:14.280
You can feel that split yourself. Open Clawed, paste a tiny set of numbers from a class experiment,

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.560
and ask it to explain what they show, then start a new chat and ask only for a brand-new

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.880
experiment idea, and notice which reply feels stronger.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.340
The same company is testing a quieter habit inside Claude, and it is the closest thing

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:29.460
AI has to sleep.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:34.580
Your brain does a strange thing at night, even after you shut your eyes, pieces of the day

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:38.080
float back, a joke from lunch, a tricky math problem.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.220
That replay is how your mind files what matters and lets the rest fade.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.120
And,thropic engineers are trying a similar loop.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:50.100
Instead of leaving every chat as one giant messy notebook, they give the model stretches

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.420
of idle time when no one is talking to it.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.840
During those stretches, the AI re-reads its own earlier logs, spots mistakes it made,

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.060
and re-write scattered notes into shorter, cleaner summaries it can use later.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:07.460
The team calls this step dreaming because it happens when the model is not actively answering

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:11.200
anyone, the way your brain does most of its organizing while you sleep.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.700
Deep. Step 1 is capture. Every conversation is experienced, like stuffing worksheets into

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:22.260
a backpack all day. Step 2 is the quiet. Nobody is asking a question, so the model is

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.860
not performing. Step 3 is replay and tidy. It sorts the backpack, throws out the crumpled

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:33.240
scraps, and keeps a one-page version of what actually mattered. Step 4 is carry forward.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:39.080
The next morning it does not start from a blank page. That last step is the point. Without

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.740
Without it, every new chat is like opening Minecraft and getting a brand new world, with

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.080
none of yesterday's builds.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:49.480
With it, Claude can keep useful patterns across many separate conversations.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.560
Early tests show the method already helps the model stay consistent on longer projects

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.120
that stretch over several days.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:02.700
The same basic loop, capture experience, then replay and tidy it up, shows up in human memory

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.940
research and in this new AI design.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:09.300
Once you see idle time as filing time, the whole trick feels ordinary.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:10.720
It is not magic.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:13.180
It is homework club for the model's own notes.

